ive priorities. H30 is also deferred: its point estimate exceeded the unstable outcome_006 threshold, but its interval included zero and outcome_006 has a near-zero mean, making the 10% relative benchmark scientifically unstable.\n\nThe accepted claim set remains empty. The accumulated evidence across binary and balanced-predictor marginal screens is broadly null or inconsistent. H19 was rejected after its independent validation reversed direction and was not clinically meaningful. H13 is now also rejected and closed after validation ruled out clinically meaningful stronger dependency. Many rejected claims have upper bounds below the clinical benchmark, while a larger number remain unresolved because intervals still allow effects at or above 10%, especially for H3, H6, H7, H8, H15-H18, H27-H34, H37-H39, and H52. None of these have sufficient evidence to support acceptance. The most informative next step is to complete the registered interaction screen H40-H51 among the three nearly balanced predictors feature_002, feature_034, and feature_060 for outcomes with stable absolute thresholds: outcome_011, outcome_013, outcome_015, and outcome_016. These tests can reveal context-specific associations that marginal main effects may mask. If the interaction screen is also null, I would close the balanced binary strategy and pivot to multi-level categorical contrasts or carefully defined numeric/subgroup hypotheses, while still treating all associations as dataset-specific rather than causal or generalizable."
